  },
  {
    "id": "zinus-euro-top-hybrid",
    "name": "Zinus 12 Inch Euro Top Pocket Spring Hybrid Mattress",
    "category": "12-inch Euro-Top Hybrid, Medium",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Much lower price than the other picks",
      "Euro top over individually wrapped iCoil springs",
      "Listing gives a 500-lb weight limit for the queen",
      "Cover the listing describes as cool to the touch, OEKO-TEX certified",
      "10-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Thinner comfort layers than the premium picks",
      "No stated edge reinforcement",
      "Twin size is limited to 250 lbs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible budget hybrid for a guest room or a lighter sleeper who wants a soft top over coils.",
    "description": "Not every mattress needs to be a long-term investment. If you are furnishing a guest room for visiting grandchildren, setting up a bed for a parent who is moving in for a few months, or simply working to a tight budget, the Zinus 12 Inch Euro Top Pocket Spring Hybrid is a sensible place to start. It puts a quilted euro top and supportive foams over individually wrapped iCoil pocket springs, for a 12-inch mattress the Amazon listing describes as medium. The listing gives a 500-lb weight limit for the queen, a cover that feels cool to the touch, OEKO-TEX certification and a 10-year limited warranty. Wrapped coils move independently, which helps limit motion transfer between partners compared with a traditional innerspring. Where it falls short of the premium picks is in the details that matter most as we age. The comfort layers are thinner, so heavier sleepers and side sleepers with sore shoulders may feel the coils. The listing does not describe a reinforced edge, so the sides may be softer to sit on. And the twin is limited to 250 lbs. Zinus also uses very similar names across several hybrids, so check the model number on the listing before ordering. For a primary bed you will use every night for years, the DreamCloud Premier is worth the extra money."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Mattress for Seniors",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-mattress-for-seniors/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best mattress for seniors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most older sleepers, the DreamCloud Premier 13-inch Hybrid is the best mattress for seniors. It is medium-firm with a cushioned euro top, a reinforced edge for sitting and standing, and pocketed coils that make changing position easier than all-foam. Couples should look at the Nectar Premier Hybrid, and hot sleepers at the Purple Restore Hybrid."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What firmness of mattress is best for elderly people?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most older sleepers are comfortable on a medium to medium-firm mattress. That range cushions the shoulders and hips while keeping the spine supported and the surface easy to move on. Side sleepers with sore joints may prefer medium or softer; back sleepers often prefer medium-firm. A very firm mattress can create pressure points and is not automatically better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best mattress height for seniors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best height lets you sit on the edge with your feet flat on the floor and your knees at about a right angle. For many adults the top of the mattress lands around 20 to 25 inches from the floor, but measure your own knee height. Remember to add the frame, foundation or adjustable base to the mattress thickness."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is memory foam good for older people?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Memory foam cushions joints and absorbs motion well, which many older sleepers like. Its downside is a slow response that can make rolling over or sitting up harder, and it can sleep warm. A hybrid with a memory foam layer over coils, or a responsive surface like Purple's gel grid, often gives a better balance for older sleepers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do seniors need an adjustable bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No one needs one, but many older sleepers find an adjustable base helpful for reading, watching television or sitting up before getting out of bed. If you add one, choose a mattress the maker says is compatible, usually a hybrid with pocketed coils or an all-foam bed. Check the combined height so the bed is not too high to get into."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should seniors replace their mattress?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many mattresses last around seven to ten years, but replace yours when it stops supporting you. Signs include visible sagging, a dip you roll into, a collapsed edge, waking stiff and feeling better once you are up, or sleeping better on other beds. Rotating the mattress every few months can help it wear evenly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What mattress is best for seniors with back pain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many people with lower back discomfort prefer a medium-firm hybrid that keeps the hips level with the shoulders, such as the DreamCloud Premier or the firm Brooklyn Bedding Signature Hybrid. A mattress is not a treatment for pain, though, so treat comfort as personal and use the full sleep trial to judge it before you decide."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
