        <div id="adjustable-base" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Adjustable-Base Compatibility: What to Check
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many older sleepers add an adjustable base, raising the head to read or watch television, or raising the head or feet for comfort. A base that sits you up can also make getting out of bed easier. The mattress has to bend with it, so compatibility matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Mattresses Bend Well</h3>

          <p>
            All-foam mattresses bend most easily. Hybrids with individually pocketed coils usually work too, because each coil moves on its own. Traditional innersprings with connected coils generally do not. Thinner mattresses bend more easily than very thick ones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Maker's Statement</h3>

          <p>
            Do not assume. Some makers state compatibility clearly, some say nothing. Nectar removes the doubt on Amazon by selling the Premier Hybrid bundled with its own adjustable base. Brooklyn Bedding does not state compatibility for the Signature Hybrid on its product page, so ask before ordering. An unsupported setup can affect the warranty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Split Sizes for Couples</h3>

          <p>
            If partners want different positions, you need a split king (two twin XL mattresses side by side) or a split base. Purple and Brooklyn Bedding both list split sizes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Base Height</h3>

          <p>
            Adjustable bases often sit higher than a simple frame. Add the base height to the mattress height before buying, and check the result against your comfortable sitting height.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Temperature: Sleeping Cooler Through the Night
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many older adults find their sleep is disturbed by feeling too warm or too cold. A mattress cannot regulate your temperature, but it can avoid trapping heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrids Sleep Cooler Than All-Foam</h3>

          <p>
            A coil unit is mostly air, so heat has somewhere to go. Dense memory foam holds heat closer to the body. Every pick in this guide is a hybrid for that reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Feel the Cold</h3>

          <p>
            Not everyone sleeps hot. Some older sleepers feel the cold more at night, and a very cool-touch cover can feel chilly on a winter evening. If that is you, skip the paid cooling upgrades, choose a quilted or euro top that feels warmer to the touch, and handle temperature with layered bedding you can add or remove. A cooling cover is easy to buy and impossible to remove later, so only pay for one if you know you run warm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cooling Covers and Comfort Layers</h3>

          <p>
            Brooklyn Bedding's GlacioTex covers and Nectar's cooling fibers are designed to feel cool to the touch. Purple's grid takes a different approach, with open channels for air. These help modestly; they will not cool a warm room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Bedroom Matters Too</h3>

          <p>
            Breathable sheets, a breathable <a href="/best-mattress-protector/">mattress protector</a> and a cool room make more difference than any cover. In dry winter air some people sleep better with a <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom humidifier</a>, and in humid summers with a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom dehumidifier</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motion-isolation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Motion Isolation for Couples
          </h2>

          <p>
            Couples often sleep on different schedules as they get older. One partner may get up several times in the night, and the other may be a lighter sleeper than before. A mattress that absorbs movement can help both sleep better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Memory Foam Absorbs the Most Movement</h3>

          <p>
            Memory foam soaks up motion well, which is why the Nectar Premier Hybrid suits couples. The trade-off is the slower feel when changing position. If one partner finds memory foam hard to move on and the other wants a quiet bed, a hybrid with a thinner memory foam layer over pocketed coils, like the DreamCloud Premier, is a reasonable middle ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pocketed Coils Beat Connected Coils</h3>

          <p>
            In a traditional innerspring, the coils are linked and a movement on one side travels across the bed. In a pocketed coil unit, each spring moves on its own. All six picks use wrapped or encased coils.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bed Size Matters as Much as Construction</h3>

          <p>
            A queen gives each partner 30 inches of width. A king gives 38. More space means less contact and less disturbance, and it leaves room to sleep away from the edge. If partners need different firmness or one wants to raise the head of the bed, a split king lets each side choose its own mattress feel and base position.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mattress-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hybrid, Memory Foam or Innerspring: Which Type Suits Older Sleepers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The type of mattress decides most of how it feels: how much it cushions, how easy it is to move on, how warm it sleeps and how long the edge holds up. For most older sleepers we recommend a hybrid, and every pick in this guide is one, but it is worth knowing why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid</h3>

          <p>
            A hybrid puts foam comfort layers over a unit of individually wrapped coils. The foam cushions shoulders and hips; the coils give support, bounce and airflow. Coils also make it easier to build a firm edge, which is why hybrids usually beat all-foam beds for sitting and standing. Most hybrids with pocketed coils also work on adjustable bases, though you should still check the maker's guidance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">All-Foam and Memory Foam</h3>

          <p>
            All-foam mattresses are lighter, quieter and often cheaper, and they absorb motion very well. The trade-offs for older sleepers are a slower surface that can make rolling over and sitting up harder, a softer edge, and more heat retention. They can suit lighter sleepers who rarely sit on the edge of the bed and who sleep cool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Traditional Innerspring</h3>

          <p>
            Older-style innersprings with connected coils feel bouncy and sleep cool, but they transfer motion across the bed, often have thin comfort layers, and generally do not bend well on an adjustable base. If you have had one for years and liked it, a hybrid will feel familiar but quieter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Latex and Gel Grids</h3>

          <p>
            Latex and Purple's GelFlex Grid both sit between foam and coils in feel: cushioning, but quick to spring back. Many people who feel stuck in memory foam find them easier to move on. They are worth trying if ease of movement is your main concern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-and-trial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Delivery, Setup and the Sleep Trial
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most mattresses on Amazon arrive compressed in a box, and the box is heavy: a queen hybrid often weighs 80 to 120 lbs. Plan ahead so nobody gets hurt lifting it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Get Help Unboxing</h3>

          <p>
            Ask a family member or neighbor to help carry the box and unroll the mattress on the frame. Cut the plastic carefully, and allow the time the maker suggests for it to expand, often 24 to 72 hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Removing the Old Mattress</h3>

          <p>
            Some sellers offer old-mattress removal for a fee. If not, many towns run bulk pickup or recycling. Arrange this before the new one arrives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Trial</h3>

          <p>
            Most makers ask you to sleep on the new bed for about 30 nights before starting a return. Mark the end date on a calendar. If you are still waking stiff after a month or two, use the trial.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Replace a Mattress
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many mattresses last somewhere around seven to ten years, but the right time to replace one is when it stops supporting you, not when a date passes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Is Time</h3>

          <p>
            Visible sagging or body impressions, a dip you roll into, waking stiff and feeling better after getting up, or sleeping better on a hotel or guest bed are all signs. So is an edge that has collapsed enough to make sitting on it awkward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Start</h3>

          <p>
            For a broader comparison of types and brands, see our main <a href="/best-mattress/">best mattress guide</a> and our roundup of the <a href="/best-mattress-brands/">best mattress brands</a>. Our guide to <a href="/mattress-prices-and-deals/">mattress prices and deals</a> explains when brands discount.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Bed That Sits Too Low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin mattress on a low frame makes standing up harder every morning. Measure your comfortable sitting height and add up frame, foundation and mattress before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Firmest Mattress for Your Back</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Very firm is not automatically better. Many older sleepers find a medium to medium-firm bed more comfortable on the shoulders and hips.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Edge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you sit on the side of the bed to dress or steady yourself, a soft edge sinks and tips you forward. Choose reinforced edge support.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Any Mattress Works on an Adjustable Base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Connected-coil innersprings and some thick mattresses do not bend well. Check the maker's statement or buy a bundled set.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Unboxing a Heavy Mattress Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A boxed queen hybrid can weigh 80 to 120 lbs. Ask for help, or pay for delivery and setup if it is offered.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

