        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most older sleepers, the DreamCloud Premier 13-inch Hybrid is the best mattress for seniors. It is medium-firm with a cushioned euro top, a reinforced edge for sitting and standing, and pocketed coils that make changing position easier than all-foam. Couples should look at the Nectar Premier Hybrid, and hot sleepers at the Purple Restore Hybrid.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What firmness of mattress is best for elderly people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most older sleepers are comfortable on a medium to medium-firm mattress. That range cushions the shoulders and hips while keeping the spine supported and the surface easy to move on. Side sleepers with sore joints may prefer medium or softer; back sleepers often prefer medium-firm. A very firm mattress can create pressure points and is not automatically better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress height for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best height lets you sit on the edge with your feet flat on the floor and your knees at about a right angle. For many adults the top of the mattress lands around 20 to 25 inches from the floor, but measure your own knee height. Remember to add the frame, foundation or adjustable base to the mattress thickness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is memory foam good for older people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Memory foam cushions joints and absorbs motion well, which many older sleepers like. Its downside is a slow response that can make rolling over or sitting up harder, and it can sleep warm. A hybrid with a memory foam layer over coils, or a responsive surface like Purple's gel grid, often gives a better balance for older sleepers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do seniors need an adjustable bed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No one needs one, but many older sleepers find an adjustable base helpful for reading, watching television or sitting up before getting out of bed. If you add one, choose a mattress the maker says is compatible, usually a hybrid with pocketed coils or an all-foam bed. Check the combined height so the bed is not too high to get into.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should seniors replace their mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many mattresses last around seven to ten years, but replace yours when it stops supporting you. Signs include visible sagging, a dip you roll into, a collapsed edge, waking stiff and feeling better once you are up, or sleeping better on other beds. Rotating the mattress every few months can help it wear evenly.
              </p>
            </div>

